<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kempten</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Kempten im Allgäu</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kempten is a city on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iller">Iller</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allgau">Allgäu</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>, and one of the oldest towns in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, on the site of Roman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambodunum">Cambodunum</a>. For centuries it was divided between the Catholic prince-abbey of Kempten and the Protestant Free Imperial City, until both passed to Bavaria in 1802–1803 and were merged into one town. It remains the main town of the Allgäu.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kempten.jpg" alt="Kempten" width="320" height="265" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Hilarmont · CC BY-SA 3.0 de · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Basilika_St._Lorenz_Kempten_(Foto_Hilarmont).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Kempten is the largest town of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allgau">Allgäu</a>, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swabia">Swabia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>. It is one of the oldest urban settlements in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. The area was possibly first settled by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/celts">Celts</a>, and the Greek geographer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strabo">Strabo</a> mentions a town of the Celtic Estiones named Kambodunon, which is considered the oldest written reference to any German city. No archaeological evidence has yet been found that this Celtic settlement existed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In 15 BCE Roman troops led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nero-claudius-drusus">Nero Claudius Drusus</a> and his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiberius">Tiberius</a> conquered and destroyed the existing Celtic settlement. The town, Latinized as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambodunum">Cambodunum</a>, was rebuilt on a classical Roman plan with baths, a forum and temples, first in wood and then in stone after a fire that destroyed almost the whole town in 69. It possibly served as capital of the province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raetia">Raetia</a> in the first century before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augsburg">Augsburg</a> took that role. The Alemanni destroyed it in 233, and the settlement moved to the Burghalde hill above the river <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iller">Iller</a>. In the middle of the 5th century the last Roman troops left and the Alemanni took the town over entirely.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a> destroyed the town again in 683, because it had supported an uprising against the Frankish kingdom. Around 700 two Benedictine monks from the Abbey of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-gall">Saint Gall</a>, Magnus von <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fussen">Füssen</a> and Theodor, founded Kempten Abbey, the first monastery in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allgau">Allgäu</a>. It became one of the most privileged of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carolingian-empire">Frankish Empire</a> with the support of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a>'s wife Hildegard. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magyars">Magyar</a> raids, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ulrich-of-augsburg">Ulrich of Augsburg</a>, who was also abbot, began rebuilding the monastery and the town in 941.</p><p>In 1213 Emperor Frederick II made the abbots members of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reichstag">Reichstag</a> and granted the Abbot of Kempten the title of Duke. In 1289 King Rudolf I granted privileges to the settlement in the valley, making it the Free Imperial City of Kempten. The last property rights of the abbots in the city were sold in 1525. From then two independent cities of the same name lay side by side. Conflict grew when the Imperial City turned Protestant in 1527, in opposition to the Catholic abbey, and both were destroyed during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-years-war">Thirty Years' War</a>, by imperial forces and Swedish troops respectively. The abbey and the city came under Bavarian rule in 1802–03, and in 1819 the two cities were united.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Kempten had about 68,000 inhabitants in 2016. Among its sights are St Lorenz Basilica, commissioned in 1652 by the abbot Roman Giel of Gielsberg and acknowledged as the first large church built in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> after the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-years-war">Thirty Years' War</a>, the Duke-Abbots' Residence, St Mang Church and the Archaeological Park Cambodunum, where excavations in the late 19th century and again in the 1950s uncovered the foundations of the Roman town.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kempten” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>When the Latin text is used, it is normally sung to a Gregorian chant tune first known from <b>Kempten</b> Abbey around the year 1000.</blockquote><a href="/songs/veni-creator-spiritus">Veni Creator Spiritus</a></li><li><blockquote>The town of <b>Kempten</b> grew beside the Prince-Abbey of Kempten, which originally held lordship over it.</blockquote><a href="/state/free-imperial-city-of-kempten">Free Imperial City of Kempten</a></li><li><blockquote>The Benedictine abbey of <b>Kempten</b> was founded in the mid 8th century near the old Roman town of Cambodunum and enjoyed the patronage of Charlemagne's wife Hildegard.</blockquote><a href="/state/prince-abbey-of-kempten">Prince-Abbey of Kempten</a></li></ul></section>
